  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Soak the ancho pieces in hot water for 10 minutes until supple; keep the soaking water.",
      "timer_min": 10
    },
    {
      "text": "Blend the anchos, chipotle, orange juice, garlic, cumin, oregano, oil, and salt into a smooth adobo, thinning with a spoonful of the chile water if the blades stall."
    },
    {
      "text": "Toss the pork medallions and the pineapple chunks in the adobo on the sheet pan, coating everything. Rest 15 minutes while the oven heats to 425°F.",
      "timer_min": 15
    },
    {
      "text": "Spread the pork and pineapple out — crowded pieces steam instead of char — and roast 14 minutes. Pork tenderloin is safe at 145°F (63°C); pull the pan and check a thick medallion on a thermometer.",
      "timer_min": 14,
      "safety": {
        "class": "meat_whole_cut_pork",
        "temp_f": 145,
        "method": "thermometer",
        "source": "USDA FSIS safe minimum internal temperature table"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Rest the pork 5 minutes on the pan, then chop the medallions roughly — the al pastor trompo shaves its meat, and a rough chop is the sheet-pan echo of that.",
      "timer_min": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Char the pineapple edge-side against the hot pan while you toast the tortillas in a dry skillet until pliable and freckled."
    },
    {
      "text": "Build tacos: pork, pineapple, cilantro, and a hard squeeze of lime. Pass the rest of the lime at the table."
    }
  ],
  "cuisine_note": "Tacos al pastor come off a spinning trompo; the home-oven version is a sheet pan of adobo-marinated pork and pineapple roasted hot until the edges char. Same sweet-smoky-sour architecture, no trompo required.",
